{"slides":[{"k":"title","h":"WHAT should we propose?","sub":"Session 3 · Live class · Opportunities, levers and the Action Plan","note":"Welcome people as they join and ask them to switch cameras on if they can. Say in one sentence what today is about: we turn the Spend Story from Session 2 into a proposal the stakeholder can say yes to. Remind them they have seen the lessons on video, so today we will not repeat the theory: we argue about it and apply it. Start on time; latecomers catch up during the poll."},{"k":"table","h":"Today's plan","t":[["Time","Block","How"],["09:00","Welcome and fieldwork debrief","Poll, chat waterfall"],["09:30","3.1 Finding opportunities","Debate"],["10:05","3.2 Choosing the lever","Poll, breakout rooms"],["10:55","Break","10 min"],["11:05","3.3 Sizing value","Debate, poll"],["11:40","3.4 Prioritise and sequence","Debate"],["12:05","Break","10 min"],["12:15","3.5 Action Plan and Point of View","Pairs"],["12:35","Peer challenge","Breakout trios"],["12:55","Close and Fieldwork 3",""]],"note":"Walk through the plan in under a minute; times are the local time of the class, adjust them if you start later. Point out the two breaks so people can plan. Say that most of the time is for discussion and two breakout rooms, so they should keep their own stakeholder and data pack at hand. Ask them to keep the workbook open in another tab."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s3-l1","note":"Energiser: launch the poll straight away and give 60 seconds. Show the results and read them out with some humour, especially ‘your numbers are wrong’, which happens to everyone. If many have not held the playback yet, do not judge: ask them to listen to the others and book it this week. Use the result to pick who speaks first on the next slide."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Fieldwork debrief: the data playback","q":"Did your playback earn you a second meeting, or just a ‘thank you’?","b":["One sentence: what surprised your stakeholder?","What did they challenge in the data?","Did anyone ask ‘so what should we do?’","What would you present differently next time?"],"min":15,"format":"Chat waterfall","note":"Ask everyone to type their one-sentence answer in the chat but not press Enter until you say ‘go’, then all post at once. Read four or five aloud, choosing a mix of good and difficult experiences, and invite two people to unmute and tell the story in one minute each. Close the debrief with the key link: the best outcome of a playback is the question ‘so what should we do?’, and today we build the answer. Keep it to 15 minutes even if the energy is high."},{"k":"section","n":"3.1","h":"Finding opportunities","sub":"Spend, renewals, the pipeline and the Group","note":"Transition: in the video they saw the four sources of opportunities: spend analysis, renewal calendar, demand pipeline, market and Group. Do not re-teach them; say that we will test whether we really use all four. About 35 minutes for this block."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Are we only waiting for expiry dates?","q":"‘Most of our opportunities are just contracts that happen to expire.’","b":["True in your team today?","What is lost if the calendar drives everything?","Which source do you never use?","Who in the business tells you about new projects?"],"min":12,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"Ask people to react with a thumbs up (agree) or thumbs down (disagree) and call on one person from each side. Push back on both: expiring contracts are indeed 60 to 70% of a typical pipeline, but proactive initiatives from spend analysis and the business's own projects are where Procurement looks like a partner instead of an administrator. Ask the agree side what their stakeholder would think if they only ever heard from Procurement at renewal time."},{"k":"stats","h":"The Group lens: we all buy the same","s":[["10","entities buying the same categories"],["≈ 350","IT services suppliers across the Group"],["≈ 2%","of spend under a Group contract"],["≈ 70%","of value from common standards, not volume"]],"note":"These are anonymised Group figures from the video lessons, rounded. The point: the biggest opportunity is often not bundling volume but buying the same way, with common specifications, rate cards and benchmarks. Ask: ‘If 2% is under a Group contract, what is stopping the other 98%?’ Collect two or three answers and keep them for the synergy lever in 3.2."},{"k":"discuss","h":"The opportunity nobody asked for","q":"Name one opportunity in your data that your stakeholder has never asked about.","b":["Which source did it come from?","Would the stakeholder see it as help or as interference?","What evidence do you already have?"],"min":8,"format":"Pairs in breakout rooms","note":"Open breakout rooms in pairs for five minutes: each person names one opportunity and the partner asks ‘why would your stakeholder care?’. Back in plenary, ask two pairs to share the best idea they heard from their partner, not their own. This feeds their Practice 3.1 long list: remind them the long list needs at least eight items, tagged by source, before Session 4."},{"k":"section","n":"3.2","h":"Choosing the strategy lever","sub":"Nine options, and the conversation behind each","note":"Transition: a long list is not a plan; for each opportunity we have to choose how to act. Remind them of the provocation from the video: if your only tool is a tender, every renewal looks like a tender. About 50 minutes including the breakout."},{"k":"table","h":"The nine levers on one page","t":[["Lever","Use it when"],["Renegotiate","Supplier performs, switching is costly"],["Tender","Contract expiring, market has moved"],["Modify the service","Over-specified, unused scope"],["Demand management","Consumption grows without control"],["Consolidate","Many suppliers for similar work"],["Group synergy","Other entities buy the same"],["Restructure the contract","Uplifts, weak exit clauses"],["Partnership","Critical supplier, few alternatives"],["Keep and monitor","Low value, low risk, recent contract"]],"note":"Use this only as a reference card; do not read it out. Ask: ‘Which lever have you used in the last year that is not a tender or a renegotiation?’ and take two quick answers. If nobody answers, that is the message: most of us use two levers out of nine. Leave the slide up during the poll."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s3-l2","note":"Give 60 seconds. Show the results and do not reveal a ‘right answer’: each option has a defensible case. Ask someone who chose renegotiate and someone who chose to join the framework directly to explain in 30 seconds each. Note that the CMO distrusts Procurement after a slow tender; keep that tension for the next slide."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Is the tender the safe choice?","q":"‘For an expired contract, a full tender is always the safest choice.’","b":["Safe for whom: Procurement, the CMO or the budget?","What does the CMO remember about our last tender?","What does the Group framework change?","When is a tender the risky option?"],"min":10,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"Split the room: those who agree argue for compliance and market proof, those who disagree argue for speed and the relationship. Let them debate for six to seven minutes, intervening only to bring in the CMO's point of view. Land the point: the lever is chosen by need first, then market, contract and relationship, and a slow tender with a sceptical stakeholder can cost more trust than it saves money."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Breakout: choose the lever for Nordvia Marketing","min":25,"where":"Breakout rooms of 3–4 · Practice 3.2 in the workbook","b":["Five services: media, creative, events, research, print","Choose one lever per service and write why","Your teacher sends your room one hidden fact","Decide: does the fact change your answer?","Name a spokesperson for two minutes"],"note":"Before opening the rooms, send each room one hidden fact from the Case tab by chat or broadcast: the framework joining clause, the CMO's favourite creative agency, the events contract Communication will sign next month, the 30% research budget cut. Do not tell the other rooms what the facts are. Visit each room for a minute; if a room is stuck, ask them to start with the service their fact concerns. Broadcast a five-minute warning."},{"k":"debrief","h":"What did your hidden fact change?","b":["Each room: your fact, and the lever it changed","Which service did all rooms agree on?","Where did the CMO's loyalty change the answer?","What would you say to the CMO first?"],"note":"Give each spokesperson two minutes: first the hidden fact, then the one answer it changed. Write the changed levers in the chat or on a shared screen. The lesson to draw out: the same data gives different right answers once you know the stakeholder's context, which is why the ally conversation in Fieldwork 3 matters. Then send them to the break on time."},{"k":"statement","h":"Break · back at 11:05","p":"Ten minutes. Stretch, refill, and think of one number your stakeholder would not believe.","note":"Start a visible ten-minute timer if your video tool allows it. Remind people to keep their camera off and stay in the call rather than leaving. The prompt on the slide is the hook for 3.3: you will ask for that number when you come back."},{"k":"section","n":"3.3","h":"Sizing value beyond savings","sub":"Ranges, assumptions and five dimensions","note":"Welcome them back and ask one or two people for the number their stakeholder would not believe. Say: a number nobody believes is worse than no number. About 35 minutes for this block."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Is cost avoidance real money?","q":"‘Cost avoidance is not a saving. Finance will never count it.’","b":["Supplier asks +8%, we close at +3%: what did we deliver?","Who in your entity decides what counts?","How do you make avoidance visible in the budget?","Have you ever agreed a baseline with Finance?"],"min":12,"format":"Open floor","note":"Throw the statement and wait; someone always agrees strongly. Use the Kelvar-style example: an uplift held down is money the budget did not have to find. Ask anyone who has agreed a baseline with Finance to explain how, and if nobody has, make that a concrete action for their plan. Do not let it become a complaint session about Finance: steer to what they can do before the stakeholder meeting."},{"k":"stats","h":"The Group planning assumption","s":[["≈ 6%","value per 3-year contract cycle"],["≈ 2%","cost savings"],["≈ 4%","cost avoidance"],["≈ 2/3","of expected local value is avoidance"]],"note":"Stress the wording: it is a planning estimate of value, not a target and not a budget cut. Ask: ‘If two thirds of our value is avoidance, what happens to our credibility if we only report savings?’ Take one or two answers and move on; this slide supports the previous debate."},{"k":"discuss","h":"A number or a range?","q":"The CMO asks: ‘How much will I save on media?’ Do you give one number or a range?","b":["€1.5M versus €1.2–2.0M a year","Which one starts a fight about the number?","What is the one assumption you would state?","What other value would you mention?"],"min":10,"format":"Chat waterfall","note":"Ask everyone to write their exact answer to the CMO in the chat, one sentence, and post together on ‘go’. Read three aloud: one with a single number, one with a range and one that mentions risk or speed. Point out that a range with a stated assumption keeps the conversation on the decision, while a precise number invites an argument about decimals."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s3-l3","note":"Quick poll, 45 seconds. If cost savings wins by far, ask: ‘Is that what your stakeholder says, or what we assume?’ Connect to Session 1: the stakeholder map told them what this person cares about. Tell them their one-page business case (Practice 3.3) must cover at least one dimension beyond price."},{"k":"section","n":"3.4","h":"Prioritise and sequence","sub":"Value, effort and the calendar","note":"Transition: a plan with twenty priorities has none. This block is about choosing, and about timing. About 25 minutes."},{"k":"cards","h":"Value and effort: four boxes","c":[["Quick wins","High value, low effort: two in the first 90 days"],["Major projects","High value, high effort: plan them with the stakeholder"],["Fill-ins","Low value, low effort: when capacity allows"],["Deprioritise","Low value, high effort: say no, explicitly"]],"note":"Recap the matrix quickly, they know it from the video. Ask the room: ‘In which box do most of your current requests sit?’ The honest answer is often ‘fill-ins’. Remind them of lead times: a complex tender starts 9 to 12 months before expiry, a renegotiation 4 to 6 months before."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Is saying no a sign of strength?","q":"‘Saying no to an initiative builds more trust than saying yes to everything.’","b":["When did you last say no to a stakeholder?","How do you say no without closing the door?","Who decides what is deprioritised: you or them?"],"min":8,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"Take a quick thumbs-up count, then ask for a real example of a ‘no’ that went well and one that went badly. Steer to the idea that an explicit no, with a reason and a date to revisit, is a sign of a planned pipeline. Keep it short: the next slide is the more practical dilemma."},{"k":"discuss","h":"The events contract signs next month","q":"Communication will sign a 3-year events contract on its own next month. Do you step in?","b":["Quick win or turf war?","Who do you call first: the CMO or Communication?","What do you offer instead of a delay?","What if they sign anyway?"],"min":10,"format":"Open floor","note":"This was one room's hidden fact; now everyone sees it. Let two or three people propose what they would do, then ask the room which approach the Head of Communication would accept. The usual best answer: offer to join the buying for both teams quickly, a quick win for the CMO, rather than asking to stop the signature. Underline that timing comes from the stakeholder's calendar, not only from contract dates. Send them to the second break on time."},{"k":"statement","h":"Break · back at 12:15","p":"Ten minutes. When we return: the Action Plan and the Point of View.","note":"Start the timer. Ask them to open their Action Plan template (Practice 3.5) during the break if they have not yet, because the next block uses it immediately. Check the breakout room setup for trios during the break."},{"k":"section","n":"3.5","h":"The Action Plan and the Point of View","sub":"One table and one page","note":"Remind them of the two outputs: the Action Plan is one table the stakeholder can read in five minutes, and the Point of View is one page of hypotheses that opens a discussion. The plan is not our plan for their spend; it is our proposal for their goals. About 20 minutes."},{"k":"two","h":"Hypotheses that close or open","l":{"t":"Closes the discussion","b":["‘We will tender media buying in January.’","‘You must consolidate to three agencies.’","‘The saving is €1.6M.’"]},"r":{"t":"Opens the discussion","b":["‘We believe… because…’","‘We suggest we explore…’","A range and its assumption","The decision stays with the business"]},"hi":"r","note":"Read the left column in the voice of a confident buyer, then ask: ‘How would a new CMO who distrusts Procurement hear this?’ Show that the right column says the same thing but leaves the decision with the business. Do not add more theory; the next slide puts them to work."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Rewrite it for the CMO","q":"Turn ‘We will tender media buying in January’ into a hypothesis the CMO wants to discuss.","b":["Start from his goal: funding the digital campaign","Use ‘We believe… because… We suggest…’","One sentence, no jargon"],"min":10,"format":"Pairs in breakout rooms","note":"Pairs for five minutes, each pair writes one sentence and posts it in the chat when they return. Read the best three aloud and ask the room to vote with a reaction for the one the CMO would answer. A strong version starts with his goal, for example ‘You need budget for the digital campaign; we believe part of it is in your media spend.’ Remind them their own Point of View needs three to five hypotheses like this."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s3-l4","note":"Use this poll to warm up the peer challenge. Show the results and say that each of these objections will come up in Session 4 role-plays. Ask everyone to keep in mind the objection they fear most for the trio exercise."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Peer challenge: the devil's advocate panel","min":20,"where":"Breakout trios · your own Action Plan","b":["Five minutes: present your plan and first hypothesis","Peers play the stakeholder and Finance","Challenge hard: no defending, just listen","Write down the strongest objection","Rotate until all three have presented"],"note":"Explain the roles before opening the rooms: one peer is the stakeholder (‘why change something that works?’), the other is the Finance business partner (‘where is the baseline?’). Insist the presenter does not defend, only notes the objections, because those notes feed Session 4. Broadcast a message at minutes 7 and 14 to rotate. Back in plenary, ask two people for the objection that surprised them most."},{"k":"steps","h":"Fieldwork 3, before Session 4","s":[["Pre-align with an ally","Test the plan with a deputy, Finance partner or service owner"],["Book the meeting","60 minutes, in the two weeks after Session 4"],["Send the pre-read","Spend Story and Action Plan, three working days before"],["Bring three objections","The hardest ones: we use them in role-plays"]],"note":"Go through the four steps and stress the ally conversation: ‘I'd value your view before I take this to your boss.’ Ask each person to type in the chat the name of the role they will use as their ally. Remind them that the objections from the peer challenge count as a start for step four."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"Session 3 in five lines","b":["Look beyond expiry dates: four sources of opportunities","Choose the lever by need, market, contract and relationship","Give value as a range, beyond savings","Two quick wins first; say no explicitly","A Point of View opens the conversation"],"note":"Ask two or three people to say in the chat the one thing they will do differently next week. Read a few aloud and thank the group for the debate. Close with the next session: HOW do we align, where they rehearse the stakeholder meeting before they hold it. End on time."}],"polls":[{"id":"s3-l1","q":"At your data playback, your stakeholder's reaction was closest to…","options":["‘So what should we do?’","‘Your numbers are wrong’","‘Interesting, send me the file’","‘Why is Procurement looking at my budget?’","I have not held it yet"]},{"id":"s3-l2","q":"Nordvia media buying: €18M, contract expired 8 months ago, Group framework in another country. Your lever?","options":["Renegotiate with the incumbent","Full competitive tender","Tender with the Group framework as an option","Join the Group framework directly","Keep and monitor for now"]},{"id":"s3-l3","q":"Which value dimension moves your stakeholder most?","options":["Cost savings in the budget","Cost avoidance: the budget does not grow","Risk reduction and compliance","Quality and speed","Sustainability"]},{"id":"s3-l4","q":"Which objection do you fear most from your stakeholder?","options":["‘Why change a supplier that works?’","‘This saving is not real’","‘We have no time for a tender’","‘It is my budget, not yours’"]}]}